================================================================================
TEST 47209 -- Fibre pluggable with supported speeds
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 19:19:48-19:24:35 NZDT (tb470 time; the x230 holds NZ wall time labelled
          UTC, ~2 s behind tb470, so its log times read as NZDT; the stack clock is true UTC)
Bench:    tb470, consoles u0 (x230, DUT), u5 (stack master); Test Engineer terrenceb@terrenceb-dl
Tester:   bench-runner (by hand: tools/tb ckcon -> tools/ckcon.py with its @poll / @sleep
          directives, tools/rcdiff.py, tools/rc2cfg.py; no framework script)
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWPTCM-T47209 (Draft), objective and precondition empty, 1 step (step text and
          expected result empty)
Build:    swi_f awplus_main-20261008-57 (x230v2_28GS-awplus_main-20261008-57.rel), bootloader 6.2.40
          stk_a awplus_main-20261008-57, members 1/3/4
Configs:  swi_f.cfg (running-config before step 1; the case needs no setup), stk_a.cfg
          (running-config at the end; the stack had no setup and its one change was reverted
          at once); saved to startup: no

VERDICT: FAIL -- the AT-SPSX (1000BASE-SX) runs its one supported speed, 1000 full, with autoneg
         and fixed, and 10 / 2500 / 10000 and half duplex are rejected; but `speed 100` and
         `speed auto 100` are accepted on the SX module, the x230 then reports the port
         "connected a-full 100", and the link passes no frames in either direction
      1000 full, autonegotiated (default) ..... PASS  (current speed 1000; frames received)
      1000 full, fixed `speed 1000` ............ PASS  (a-full 1000; input counter rising)
      speed 10 / 2500 / 10000 .................. PASS  ("% port1.0.4: Unsupported speed/duplex
                                                         combination port1.0.4", status unchanged)
      duplex half .............................. PASS  ("% Duplex set failed port1.0.4")
      speed 100 / speed auto 100 ............... FAIL  (accepted, no error; "connected a-full
                                                         100"; input flat 40 s on both ends,
                                                         LLDP gone; partner still at 1000)
FAIL CONDITION: STEP 1C. Expected an unsupported speed on a 1000BASE-SX module to be refused, as
         this x230 refuses `speed 10`, `speed 2500` and `speed 10000` on the same port, or at
         least never to be reported as a working link. Instead `speed 100` and `speed auto 100`
         are accepted with no "% " line and no log error, the x230 reports the port "connected"
         at "a-full 100" / "current speed 100", and over 40 s the link carries nothing: the x230's
         input counter stays at 2740 while the stack's output counter rises 36148 -> 36191, the
         stack's input counter stays at 13770 while the x230's output counter rises 246 -> 248,
         and both LLDP neighbour entries for the link disappear. The partner meanwhile reads
         "connected a-full a-1000". It is a product failure, not the method: the same optic,
         cable and partner pass frames at 1000 immediately before and after (STEP 1A, STEP 1D),
         and the switch reports a dead link as up.
TRIED:   1. `speed 100` (19:20:02): accepted, "connected 104 a-full 100".
         2. `speed auto 100` (19:20:07): accepted, "connected 104 a-full a-100"; with `duplex
            full` added the port still read "full   a-100".
         3. `speed 100` again (19:21:14) and held ~75 s: counters and LLDP read on both ends
            (above).
         4. Control on the partner (19:23:17): the IE520's 1000BASE-SX port also accepts
            `speed 100` and reads "connected 104 a-full 100" (reverted within 3 s; frame
            passing on the IE520 was not measured).
CAVEAT:  only the AT-SPSX in x230 port1.0.4 was tested, against an AT-SPSX in the IE520 stack;
         the x230 holds no other fibre pluggable. The SPSX reports no diagnostics (no DDM), so
         optical levels were not read. Frames on the link are the stack's loop-protection
         detect frames (75 packets/s across all its ports) and LLDP from both ends; no traffic
         generator was used.

